   "text": "I read this as pirarubicin, which is doxorubicin carrying a tetrahydropyranyl group on the 4'-oxygen of the sugar. I wrote the SMILES from the known doxorubicin skeleton and confirmed each region against the drawing.\n\n- **Aglycone:** a tetracyclic anthracyclinone with a methoxy group on the aromatic ring and two quinone carbonyls. It has two phenolic OH groups and a tertiary C\u2013OH carrying a C(=O)CH2OH side chain.\n- **Sugar:** a daunosamine-type ring with an NH2 group and a methyl group, joined to the aglycone through a glycosidic O.\n- **Extra ring:** a six-membered tetrahydropyran (five carbons and one ring O) attached through an acetal carbon to the sugar's 4'-oxygen.\n\nMost stereocentres follow the wedges and hashes as I recall them in the doxorubicin SMILES. For the tetrahydropyran acetal carbon I worked out the configuration from the wedge to the sugar oxygen and got `@@`. That differs from my recollection of the database entry, which I recalled as `@`. I went with my own reading of the drawing for that centre, so it is the least certain part of the answer.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H]1[C@H]([C@H](C[C@@H](O1)O[C@H]2C[C@@](CC3=C(C4=C(C(=C23)O)C(=O)C5=C(C4=O)C=CC=C5OC)O)(C(=O)CO)O)N)O[C@@H]6CCCCO6"
